WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.180 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  we live in a world that is filled with  so much noise a 2018 Deloitte study so  the average American picks up their  phone 52 times a day now you may be  thinking and doing the math that's twice  an hour that's not so bad  but when you'd factor in the time that  you're spending sleeping eating driving  a car having sex things you should all  be doing without a phone in your hand  it's really more like once every 10  minutes  when is the last time you voluntarily  turned off your phone for 24 hours 48  hours I would say for the device in your  pocket all the alerts the dings the  bings the emails is keeping you from  living your best life and I'm not  talking about Kardashian best life I'm  talking about a life that is aligned  intentionally with your values and your  desires eight years ago I was not living  my best life I was living a good life  but it was not my best life I was  leading two teams of 45 people creating  450 pieces of editorial content each  month for 2 million Americans you might  say I was contributing to the content  problem  I was working 12 to 15 hour days trying  to manage those 45 direct reports and  every morning I would get up at 7 a.m.  the first thing I would do was check my  phone  I would then commute up to the city for  an hour have a full day commute back  down have dinner get back on my laptops  or typing away emails and every night  before I went to bed whether it was at  1:30 to 2:30 3:00 the last thing I did  every night was check my phone one last  time to see if there was an email that I  missed  that same year on a Saturday in October  I had a Friday just like the Friday that  I add a that I told you about I remember  that I was tired but I still wanted to  make that drive from the city of to the  city to get a drink with my friend for  her birthday I remember driving to the  city I remember it was a cold San  Francisco night  pretty much every night in San Francisco  was cold  I remember huddling under the heat lamp  trying to get warm  I remember feeling tired and then I  started to feel odd I started to feel  really weird and I remember I walked  over to the bar to get a glass of water  hoping that that glass of water would  somehow make me feel better and the next  thing I knew I was on the floor of the  bar I had fainted so this is a part of  this story for most people say this was  the turning point this is the moment  that I realized that my life really  needed to change that is not how this  story goes the next day I went to the  doctor and she said you are a very  normal healthy 26 year old except that  you were severely dehydrated and  exhausted so I went and I fixed those  two things i rehydrated myself I got a  little bit more sleep and I went right  back to my life my overworked exhausted  tech addicted life those next eight  years are really intense we nearly ran  out of money twice that company I was  telling you about we raised more money  we sold that company to a much bigger  company taking our 250 person  organization and trying to integrate it  into this 250,000 person organization I  left that company I ended a really  important relationship but that year  that first dear that I was telling you  about I began a practice that has  transformed the way I live that year I  began the practice of retreating from my  life in order to move forward in my life  with greater intention and  purpose every three months right after  work on Friday I drive up into the  California hills into that beautiful  golden place I go to my favorite retreat  center silence day I get out of my car  and let the beauty and the silence begin  to fill my soul but the thing that I do  is I take out my phone and I turn it off  for the next 36 hours until Sunday  afternoon this practice has grounded me  through the storms of life it has  created space for me to simply be space  to reflect on my life and it's given my  brain 36 hours to detached from my  technology addiction  helping my brain reset just once so I  want to invite you all to join me in  retreating from your life to move  forward maybe you are feeling like I did  desperate overworked maybe for you it's  being a parent that daily grind  maybe it's laundry chores dishes repeat  maybe it's the weight of figuring out  who you want to be right now and who you  want to be in the future now I know some  of you are thinking Tiffany this is  crazy I can't take off 36 hours for my  life I know the extroverts out there are  thinking I can't be quiet for two hours  much less 36 hours I know the parents  are thinking I cannot abandon my  co-parent for the entire weekend they  will kill me when I get back so I have a  challenge for you I'm calling it the  coffee top coffee shop challenge I  invite you to take a small tech detox of  just two hours this weekend this  Saturday or Sunday go to a coffee shop  and turn off your phone for two hours  and during that time you can do the  three things that I do whenever I go on  my retreats first I turn off my phone  you'll have already done that to I begin  to reflect on the last three  not your whole life just the last three  months what worked what didn't what  really sucked what do you want to do  differently and then lastly I pull out  the list of things I would love to do  with my life and I choose one thing to  do in the next three months if you don't  have that list this weekend is a perfect  time to make that list we live in a  world that is filled with so much noise  resist the urge to consume more  information and do more instead turn  inwards my senior year at Stanford I  remember reading this line that has  stuck with me to this day  Richard foster in the celebration of  discipline said the desperate need in  our world today is not for more gifted  or intelligent people but for deep  people I'm going to say it again  the desperate need in our world today is  not for more gifted people or  intelligent people but deep people  friends you are some of the most gifted  and intelligent people I know and your  life challenge will be to figure out  what is your gift to the world but  before you lead others before you change  lives change organizations and change  the world I want to invite you all to  begin by changing yourself that starts  by turning off your phone and turning  inwards  [Applause]  [Music]

